# Build context is ./api (see docker-compose.yml), so this file governs what
# `COPY . ./api/` in the Dockerfile is even able to see.
#
# Iteration 12 T2 measured the cost of not having this file: the context was
# 106 files and 809,363 bytes against 45 files and 346,978 bytes of source --
# 61 files and 57% of the bytes were host bytecode being shipped into a
# production image. `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` in the Dockerfile stops the
# container writing *new* bytecode; it has never had anything to say about
# bytecode copied in from the developer's machine.

# **Pattern syntax is `filepath.Match`, not gitignore.** Measured the hard way:
# the first version of this file wrote `__pycache__/`, rebuilt, and 6 of the 7
# bytecode directories still shipped. A bare or trailing-slash pattern matches
# only at the context root; nested directories need the `**/` prefix. The
# trailing slash does nothing at all here and is not a directory marker.

# Host bytecode. The reason this file exists.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*$py.class

# Test and type-checker caches, none of which belong in a runtime image.
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.coverage
**/htmlcov

# Virtual environments. `.venv` is where this project's host interpreter lives,
# and copying it in would shadow the wheels the image installed.
**/.venv
**/venv
**/env

# Development dependencies are deliberately never installed into the runtime
# image (see api/requirements-dev.txt's own header). Before this file they were
# still *copied* into it, which is inventory nobody asked for.
requirements-dev.txt
requirements-dev.lock

# Version control and editor leavings.
**/.git
**/.gitignore
**/.dockerignore
**/*.swp
**/*~
**/.DS_Store

# Local databases and logs. The history store lives on a volume at /data; a
# stray copy baked into a layer would be both stale and a disclosure.
**/*.db
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.log
